      "text": "To solve the problem of finding how many odd numbers between 100 and 999 have distinct digits, we will proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the structure of the number:**\n   - The number is a three-digit number, say \\( \\overline{abc} \\), where \\( a, b, \\) and \\( c \\) are its digits.\n   - The number must be odd, so \\( c \\) must be one of the digits 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.\n\n2. **Determine the choices for each digit:**\n   - The digit \\( a \\) (the hundreds place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (since it cannot be 0 for a three-digit number).\n   - The digit \\( b \\) (the tens place) can be any digit from 0 to 9.\n   - The digit \\( c \\) (the units place) must be one of the odd digits: 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of choices for each digit:**\n   - \\( a \\) has 9 possible choices (1 through 9).\n   - \\( b \\) has 10 possible choices (0 through 9).\n   - \\( c \\) has 5 possible choices (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of three-digit numbers with distinct digits:**\n   - The total number of such numbers is the product of the number of choices for each digit.\n   - Therefore, the total number of three-digit numbers with distinct digits that are odd is:\n     \\[\n     9 \\times 10 \\times 5\n     \\]\n\n5. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 10 = 90\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 5 = 450\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits is \\(\\boxed{450}\\).",
