      "text": "To solve the problem of finding how many odd numbers between 100 and 999 have distinct digits, we need to consider the constraints and systematically count the valid numbers.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Identify the form of the number**:\n   The number must be a three-digit number, so it can be represented as \\( \\overline{abc} \\), where \\( a, b, \\) and \\( c \\) are digits, and \\( a \\) cannot be 0 (since it is a three-digit number).\n\n2. **Conditions for distinct digits and oddness**:\n   - \\( a \\) must be an odd digit. The odd digits are 1, 3, 5, 7, and 9. So, \\( a \\) can be any one of these 5 digits.\n   - \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) can be any digit from 0 to 9, but they must be distinct from \\( a \\). So, \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) can each be any 9 digits (0-9), except when \\( b \\) is already chosen as \\( a \\) (which is impossible since \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) need to be distinct).\n   - The number must be odd, so the last digit \\( c \\) must be one of the odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, or 9).\n\n3. **Calculate the number of valid combinations**:\n   - **Case 1: \\( a = 1 \\)**:\n     - \\( c \\) can be any of the 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n     - \\( b \\) can be any of the 9 remaining digits (0-9, except \\( a \\) and \\( c \\)).\n     - Number of combinations: \\( 5 \\times 9 = 45 \\).\n\n   - **Case 2: \\( a = 3 \\)**:\n     - \\( c \\) can be any of the 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, but it cannot be 3).\n     - \\( b \\) can be any of the 8 remaining odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 0-9 excluding \\( a \\) and \\( c \\)).\n     - Number of combinations: \\( 5 \\times 8 = 40 \\).\n\n   - **Case 3: \\( a = 5 \\)**:\n     - \\( c \\) can be any of the 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, but it cannot be 5).\n     - \\( b \\) can be any of the 8 remaining odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 0-9 excluding \\( a \\) and \\( c \\)).\n     - Number of combinations: \\( 5 \\times 8 = 40 \\).\n\n   - **Case 4: \\( a = 7 \\)**:\n     - \\( c \\) can be any of the 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, but it cannot be 7).\n     - \\( b \\) can be any of the 8 remaining odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 0-9 excluding \\( a \\) and \\( c \\)).\n     - Number of combinations: \\( 5 \\times 8 = 40 \\).\n\n   - **Case 5: \\( a = 9 \\)**:\n     - \\( c \\) can be any of the 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, but it cannot be 9).\n     - \\( b \\) can be any of the 8 remaining odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 0-9 excluding \\( a \\) and \\( c \\)).\n     - Number of combinations: \\( 5 \\times 8 = 40 \\).\n\n4. **Sum the number of combinations**:\n   \\[\n   45 + 40 + 40 + 40 + 40 = 225\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits is \\(\\boxed{225}\\).",
